/* TERMS: [id, term, cat, intuition, analogy, formal, why-in-his-lab] */
const TERMS = [
// ---------- spectroscopy methods ----------
["slitjet","Slit-jet supersonic expansion","spec","Gas is pushed through a long narrow slit into vacuum instead of a round pinhole. The expansion cools the molecules to 10-30 K and gives the laser a long path to absorb along.","A pinhole jet is a sprinkler; a slit jet is a sheet of water from a squeegee. The sheet stays dense much further out.","Density in a slit expansion falls as 1/r rather than 1/r-squared, so the collision rate stays high far downstream, producing one to two orders of magnitude more cooling collisions before the free-molecular freeze-in region.","This is Nesbitt's signature invention (Lovejoy and Nesbitt, Rev. Sci. Instrum. 58, 807, 1987). Almost every spectroscopy paper from the group depends on it. If you understand one thing about his lab, make it this."],
["subdoppler","Sub-Doppler resolution","spec","Making spectral lines narrower than the natural Doppler width by arranging the laser so molecules barely move toward or away from it.","Listening to a train whistle from the side rather than head-on: no pitch shift because there is no velocity component along your line of sight.","Propagating the probe beam perpendicular to the expansion axis, along the slit, nearly eliminates the velocity component along the laser direction. Residual linewidths reach roughly 60 MHz in his instruments.","It is what makes tunneling splittings, hyperfine-scale perturbations and closely spaced subbands resolvable at all. He treats resolution as the precondition for understanding, not a nice-to-have."],
["directabs","Direct absorption spectroscopy","spec","Measure how much laser light the sample removes, rather than detecting fluorescence or ions.","Weighing a letter by the difference in the scale reading, not by looking at the ink.","Beer-Lambert detection of fractional absorbance, typically with a reference beam and lock-in detection; noise-equivalent absorption sensitivities around 1e-7 per root hertz are achieved.","Direct absorption gives absolute intensities and hence column densities and transition strengths. For a metrologist at NIST that quantitative traceability is the point."],
["concmod","Concentration modulation spectroscopy","spec","Switch the discharge on and off rapidly and keep only the part of the signal that switches with it.","Finding a whisper in a crowded room by having the speaker whisper on a beat and filtering for that beat.","The discharge voltage is modulated and the absorption signal demodulated at that frequency, so only species whose concentration depends on the discharge contribute.","This is the trick that pulls a radical's absorption out from under a precursor band that may be a thousand times stronger. Introduced in the group as Davis, Farnik, Uy and Nesbitt."],
["dischargejet","Discharge slit jet","spec","Strike an electrical discharge right at the slit so reactive species are created and immediately cooled by the expansion.","A welding arc inside a wind tunnel: you make the hot fragments and freeze them in the same breath.","A glow discharge of order 500 V is sustained on the slit jaws; fragments formed in the discharge undergo many cooling collisions downstream, reaching rotational temperatures of 10-30 K.","Everything he knows about phenyl, propargyl, cyclobutyl, HOCO and the ammonium cations comes out of this source. It is simultaneously a synthesis method and a cooling method."],
["dfg","Difference-frequency generation (DFG)","inst","Mix two laser beams in a special crystal; the crystal emits light at the difference of their frequencies, landing in the mid-infrared.","Two tuning forks beating against each other to produce a slow pulsation you can hear.","Second-order nonlinear mixing in a phase-matched crystal produces a field at the frequency difference of pump and signal, with linewidth inherited from the parent lasers.","The classic workhorse of his 3 micron CH- and OH-stretch spectroscopy. Very narrow, but power-starved — which is exactly the limitation the new OPO removes."],
["opo","Optical parametric oscillator (OPO)","inst","A crystal in a mirror cavity that splits one strong pump photon into two lower-energy photons, one of which is the mid-infrared light you want.","One large banknote exchanged for two smaller ones; you keep the one you need and the cavity recycles the other.","A singly-resonant cw OPO: a periodically-poled lithium niobate fanout crystal inside a four-mirror ring cavity resonant at the signal wavelength, pumped by a narrow-linewidth fiber laser.","The 2025 'JILA OPO' delivers over 2.5 W from 3-4 microns with the idler tunable across 2400-3400 cm-1 — roughly a thousandfold power increase over DFG. This is the newest and least-known thing about his lab."],
["ppln","Periodically poled lithium niobate (PPLN) / fanout crystal","inst","A crystal whose internal structure is stamped in alternating stripes so the nonlinear conversion stays in step along its whole length. A 'fanout' design has the stripe spacing vary across the crystal so you tune by sliding it sideways.","A row of swing-pushers who each give a shove at exactly the right moment; the fanout lets you choose a row with different timing by stepping to the side.","Quasi-phase-matching: periodic reversal of the nonlinear coefficient compensates phase-velocity mismatch, with the poling period setting the phase-matched wavelength. A fanout grating provides a continuous spatial gradient in period.","The 50 mm fanout PPLN crystal is the heart of the JILA OPO and the reason it tunes broadly without changing crystals."],
["comb","Optical frequency comb","inst","A laser that emits thousands of exactly evenly spaced colours at once, each one sharp enough to use as a ruler mark.","A picket fence of perfectly spaced pickets laid across the spectrum — you read a molecule's position against the pickets.","A mode-locked laser produces a spectrum of discrete modes at frequencies f(n) = n times f(rep) plus f(ceo); stabilizing both radio-frequency parameters makes every optical mode absolutely known.","The Ye-group tool he collaborates with. It is how C60's spectrum and the breath biomarkers were measured: broad coverage and absolute frequency accuracy at the same time."],
["cedfcs","Cavity-enhanced direct frequency comb spectroscopy (CE-DFCS)","inst","Put the comb inside a high-finesse mirror cavity so each colour passes through the sample thousands of times, then read all the colours at once.","A hall of mirrors that makes a thin pane of tinted glass look deeply coloured.","Each comb tooth is coupled to a corresponding cavity resonance; effective path length scales with cavity finesse, delivering extreme absorption sensitivity simultaneously across the comb bandwidth.","This is the engine behind both the C60 work and the breathalyzer: broadband multi-species detection at trace concentrations without sacrificing resolution."],
["buffergas","Buffer-gas cooling","spec","Cool a big molecule by letting it collide with a cloud of cold helium instead of expanding it through a nozzle.","Cooling a hot stone by burying it in snow rather than blowing on it.","Momentum- and energy-transfer collisions with a cryogenic inert gas thermalize translational and rotational degrees of freedom; the 2025 C60 work reached 30 K, the lowest for that molecule.","It is the only practical way to get a molecule as large and fragile as C60 cold enough that low-J rotational levels are populated — which is where the symmetry physics lives."],
["laserdesorb","Laser desorption source","spec","Blast a solid sample with a laser pulse to launch intact molecules into the gas phase without cooking them.","Flicking a single grain off a pile with a pin rather than heating the whole pile until it smokes."," Pulsed laser ablation or desorption of a solid target entrains neutral molecules into a carrier or buffer gas with minimal fragmentation.","Used with buffer-gas cooling to get C60 into the gas phase for the 2025 Bose-Einstein statistics measurement; an oven alone risks decomposition and gives poorer control."],
["vmi","Velocity map imaging (VMI)","nano","Project charged particles onto a detector so that where they land tells you how fast and in what direction they left.","Shooting paint pellets at a wall from a spinning nozzle — the splatter pattern encodes the speeds and angles.","Electrostatic lenses map the initial velocity vector of ions or electrons onto position on a 2D imaging detector, independent of birthplace within the interaction volume.","Used in his nanoscience program for angle- and momentum-resolved photoelectron measurements from single plasmonic nanoparticles."],
["mppe","Multiphoton photoemission microscopy","nano","Use a strong ultrafast laser to kick electrons out of one nanoparticle at a time, and image where they come from.","Shaking a tree with precisely timed pushes until fruit falls, then noting which branch each piece fell from.","Several photons are absorbed coherently to exceed the work function; emission yield scales nonlinearly with intensity and is strongly enhanced where the plasmonic near field is largest.","This is how Grubisic, Schweikhard, Baker and Nesbitt showed that near-field enhancement, not far-field intensity, governs emission from single Au nanorods."],
["tcspc","Time-correlated single photon counting (TCSPC)","bio","Record the arrival time of each individual photon relative to the laser pulse, then build up a histogram.","Timing every single raindrop that hits a sensor rather than measuring how wet the ground gets.","Each detected photon is time-tagged with picosecond resolution relative to the excitation pulse; the accumulated histogram reconstructs the fluorescence decay.","Used across both the nanoscience and biophysics programmes — quantum-dot lifetimes and single-molecule FRET photon streams come from the same detection philosophy."],
["smfret","Single-molecule FRET (smFRET)","bio","Attach two dyes to one molecule; how much energy passes from one to the other tells you how far apart they are, in real time.","Two walkie-talkies whose signal strength tells you the distance between two people walking around a building.","Forster resonance energy transfer efficiency depends on donor-acceptor separation as one over one-plus-(r/R0)-to-the-sixth, giving nanometre-scale distance sensitivity in the 2-8 nm range.","The whole biophysics programme. High FRET equals folded, low FRET equals unfolded; the dwell times in each state give folding and unfolding rate constants directly."],
["strobe","Stroboscopic smFRET","bio","Flash the excitation light briefly within each camera frame so the molecule cannot smear out during the exposure.","Photographing a hummingbird with a flash instead of a long exposure.","Pulsed illumination confined to a short window of each acquisition frame removes motional and transition blur, extending reliable kinetic measurement to the frame-acquisition limit.","Nicholson, Jia and Nesbitt (2021). A clean example of his instinct: identify a systematic artefact in everyone's data and remove it at the hardware level."],
["tirf","Total internal reflection fluorescence (TIRF)","bio","Illuminate only a razor-thin layer right at the glass surface, so you see the molecules stuck there and nothing from the bulk.","A spotlight that only lights the carpet, not the room.","Light incident beyond the critical angle creates an evanescent field decaying over roughly 100 nm, exciting only fluorophores within that layer.","Enables wide-field observation of many immobilized single molecules at once — the basis for camera-based smFRET and the reason camera blur mattered enough to fix."],
["ftmw","Fourier-transform microwave spectroscopy (FTMW)","spec","Hit a cold gas pulse with a short microwave burst, then listen to the molecules ringing afterwards and Fourier-transform the ringing.","Striking a bell and recording the decaying tone to learn its exact shape, instead of sweeping a tone generator to find its pitch.","A resonant cavity or chirped excitation polarizes rotational transitions; the free induction decay is digitized and transformed, giving linewidths of a few kHz.","This is your technique, not his. Its kHz-level precision resolves nuclear quadrupole hyperfine structure that his ~60 MHz infrared resolution cannot reach. That asymmetry is your argument."],
["cpftmw","Chirped-pulse FTMW","spec","A broadband version that sweeps across gigahertz in a microsecond, so you capture a huge chunk of spectrum per gas pulse.","Instead of tuning a radio dial station by station, recording the whole band at once and sorting it later.","A fast frequency chirp coherently polarizes all transitions in a broad bandwidth; the resulting free induction decay contains the entire spectral segment.","Worth knowing because it is how the rotational-spectroscopy community now surveys unknown species — and a natural thing to propose pairing with his discharge source."],
["trot","Rotational temperature (T-rot)","spec","A single number describing how many rotational levels are populated — low means the molecules are all in their lowest few spinning states.","A crowd where nearly everyone is sitting down versus one where people are standing at every height.","The temperature that best fits the Boltzmann population distribution over rotational levels, which need not equal translational or vibrational temperature in a jet.","His papers always quote it: 11 K for phenyl, 18 K for CH2OH, 19 K for cyclopentyl, 26 K for cyclobutyl. Lower T-rot means fewer lines, less congestion, cleaner assignment."],
["doppler","Doppler broadening","spec","Lines get smeared because some molecules are moving toward the laser and some away, shifting their apparent frequency.","A crowd of people all humming the same note while milling about — the chord sounds broader than one voice.","The Gaussian contribution to linewidth from the thermal velocity distribution projected onto the laser propagation direction, scaling as the square root of temperature over mass.","The enemy that the slit geometry defeats. Removing it is what turns a blob into an assignable pattern of lines."],
// ---------- structure & dynamics ----------
["vdw","van der Waals complex","struct","Two molecules held together loosely by weak attraction rather than a chemical bond — a floppy, barely-bound pair.","Two magnets separated by a thick cushion: attached, but able to wobble, slide and rotate almost freely.","A bound cluster whose intermolecular well depth is of order tens to hundreds of wavenumbers, with large-amplitude intermolecular motion and strong coupling between internal and intermolecular coordinates.","The subject of his field-defining 1988 Chemical Reviews article and of the entire Rg-HX programme. Also the subject of your own work — this is the shared ground."],
["hbond","Hydrogen bond","struct","A hydrogen atom already attached to one electronegative atom reaching toward another, forming a directional, moderately strong link.","A person holding one handrail while stretching to touch a second one — committed to the first, attracted to the second.","Defined by IUPAC (2011) as an attractive interaction between a hydrogen atom from a molecular fragment X-H, where X is more electronegative than H, and an atom or group in the same or different molecule, with evidence of bond formation.","He was a co-author on both the IUPAC Recommendations and the accompanying Technical Report defining the hydrogen bond. He helped write the definition you would be using."],
["pes","Intermolecular potential energy surface (PES)","theory","A map of how the energy of two molecules changes as you move them relative to each other — the landscape their motion happens on.","A contour map of a valley: the shape of the terrain determines where a ball can roll and how fast it rolls there.","A multidimensional function V(R, theta, phi, ...) giving the interaction energy versus intermolecular separation and orientation, from which bound states and dynamics follow by solving the nuclear Schrodinger equation.","The ultimate deliverable of the Rg-HX programme. Spectra in, potential surface out — then the surface predicts dynamics nobody measured yet."],
["threebody","Three-body (non-additive) forces","theory","When three molecules sit together, the total interaction is not simply the sum of the three pairs — the presence of the third changes the other two.","Three people in a tent: the comfort of any pair depends on where the third person is, not just on pairwise elbow room.","The non-additive contribution to the interaction energy, formally the difference between the trimer energy and the sum of pairwise dimer energies at the same geometry, arising from exchange and induction effects.","Probed directly by his Ar2HF, Ar2DF and Ar2CO2 trimer spectroscopy. This is exactly the physics of your Ne2HCl and Ne3HCl work — the overlap could not be more exact."],
["internalrotor","Internal rotor dynamics / hindered rotation","struct","Inside a complex, one partner can spin almost freely, but the other partner's presence tilts the playing field so some orientations are preferred.","A compass needle near a weak magnet: still able to swing all the way round, but reluctant to point certain ways.","Free-rotor basis states of the monomer are mixed by the anisotropic part of the intermolecular potential; the resulting internal-rotor level pattern directly encodes the angular potential.","Lascola and Nesbitt derived the angular potential for H2O rotation inside ArH2O this way. Internal-rotor structure is how he reads anisotropy out of a spectrum."],
["lam","Large-amplitude motion","struct","Motion so big that the molecule visits very different shapes, so you cannot pretend it just vibrates a little about one structure.","A hammock, not a stiff bench — the shape you sit in depends entirely on how you move.","Vibrational motion whose amplitude invalidates the harmonic, rigid-reference-geometry approximation, requiring a curvilinear or variational treatment and often producing tunneling-split level patterns.","The recurring theme of his modern radical work: ring puckering in cyclobutyl, COH torsion in CH2OH, inversion in the ammonium cations. It is where rigid-rotor spectroscopy breaks and the interesting physics starts."],
["puckering","Ring puckering","struct","A small carbon ring is not flat; it buckles out of plane, and it can buckle either way.","A tiddlywink or a bottle cap that can pop up or down but not sit perfectly flat.","An out-of-plane ring deformation coordinate, often with a double-minimum potential whose barrier height relative to the zero-point level determines whether the molecule is localized or delocalized.","Cyclobutyl (2024): a shallow double-minimum with a highly delocalized wavefunction, giving vibrationally averaged C2v symmetry. Cyclopentyl: a ~3.7 kcal/mol barrier that localizes the wavefunction instead. Same coordinate, opposite outcomes — that contrast is his result."],
["tunneling","Tunneling splitting","quantum","A molecule can get from one equivalent shape to another by passing through a barrier it has not got the energy to climb, and this splits each energy level into a pair.","Two identical rooms connected by a wall thin enough to talk through; the shared conversation splits every note into two.","Coupling between localized states in a symmetric double-minimum potential lifts their degeneracy by an amount set by the barrier penetration integral, producing a symmetric/antisymmetric pair.","CH2OH shows transitions from both tunneling states; cyclopentyl's splitting is driven below 1 MHz by its larger barrier. The size of the splitting is a direct, quantitative readout of barrier height."],
["predissoc","Rotational and vibrational predissociation","struct","A weakly bound complex given a bit of internal excitation falls apart — and how fast depends on exactly which motion you excited.","A knot that unties quickly if you tug one strand and hardly at all if you tug another.","A bound level coupled to a dissociative continuum acquires a finite lifetime, observed as homogeneous line broadening; rates depend on the specific vibrational or rotational level, i.e. they are mode-specific.","Lovejoy and Nesbitt showed mode-specific and direct rotational predissociation in HeHF, HeDF and HeHCl, and J-dependent predissociation in NeHF. Linewidth becomes a chemical rate measurement."],
["ivr","Intramolecular vibrational redistribution (IVR)","struct","Energy put into one vibration of a molecule leaks into all the others, and the molecule effectively forgets where you put it.","Dropping dye into one corner of a pool and watching it spread until the whole pool is faintly coloured.","Anharmonic and Coriolis couplings mix a bright zeroth-order state with a dense bath of dark states, fragmenting the spectral line and establishing a timescale for energy flow.","Nesbitt and Field's 1996 review on IVR has around 860 citations. He also sees it experimentally: the cyclopentyl spectrum shows IVR fragmentation while cyclobutyl's does not."],
["coriolis","Coriolis coupling","theory","Rotation and vibration talk to each other: a spinning molecule feels its own vibrations differently, which mixes the two kinds of motion.","Trying to walk in a straight line across a spinning merry-go-round — your path curves because of the rotation.","A rotation-vibration interaction term, linear in the angular momentum components, that couples vibrational states of appropriate symmetry and perturbs their rotational structure.","A standard complication in his band analyses. Unexplained rotational-structure anomalies are usually Coriolis or a Fermi resonance, and distinguishing them is a routine part of a fit."],
["fermi","Fermi resonance","theory","Two vibrations that happen to have nearly the same energy mix together and push each other apart, borrowing each other's intensity.","Two nearly identically tuned guitar strings next to each other: pluck one and both ring, with a shifted beat.","Anharmonic coupling between near-degenerate vibrational states of the same symmetry produces mixed eigenstates with shifted energies and redistributed transition intensities.","Essential for interpreting CH-stretch manifolds in hydrocarbon radicals, where stretch and bend-overtone states crowd together."],
["watson","Watson asymmetric-top Hamiltonian","theory","The standard algebraic recipe for predicting the rotational energy levels of a lopsided molecule, including how it stretches as it spins.","A tailored formula for the ringing frequencies of an irregular bell, with correction terms for how it flexes when struck hard.","A reduced effective rotational Hamiltonian in A- or S-reduction, parameterized by rotational constants A, B, C and quartic and sextic centrifugal distortion terms, fitted to observed transition frequencies.","The workhorse fit in essentially every paper. When he says 'sixty-one trimer transitions are assigned and fit to a Watson asymmetric top Hamiltonian', this is it."],
["rotconst","Rotational constants A, B, C","struct","Three numbers that say how hard a molecule is to spin about each of its three axes — and therefore how its mass is laid out.","The three numbers describing how a figure skater's spin rate differs depending on which way they tumble.","A, B and C are inversely proportional to the principal moments of inertia; fitting them to a spectrum recovers the molecule's vibrationally averaged geometry.","The direct bridge between your microwave work and his infrared work: you measure ground-state constants to kHz precision, he measures the change on vibrational excitation."],
["centrifugal","Centrifugal distortion","struct","A fast-spinning molecule stretches slightly, so its energy levels drift from the perfect rigid pattern.","A spinning ice skater's arms creeping outward as they go faster.","Higher-order terms in the rotational Hamiltonian, scaling as powers of the angular momentum, that account for the flexibility of the molecular framework.","For floppy van der Waals complexes these terms are large and physically meaningful — they report on the softness of the intermolecular bond rather than being a mere fitting nuisance."],
["tops","Spherical, symmetric and asymmetric tops","struct","Molecules sorted by how symmetric their mass distribution is: all three moments equal, two equal, or all different.","A basketball, a rugby ball, and a shoe.","Classification by the principal moments of inertia: spherical (all equal, e.g. C60), symmetric (two equal, e.g. NH3), asymmetric (all different, e.g. H2O).","C60 is a spherical top, which is exactly why its low-J behaviour is so exotic: enormous degeneracies and tiny rotational constants mean the quantum symmetry effects are not washed out."],
["nss","Nuclear spin statistics","quantum","Identical nuclei in a molecule must be swapped without changing the physics, and that requirement wipes out some rotational levels entirely.","A dance where certain formations are simply forbidden by the rules, so you never see them however the music goes.","Permutation symmetry of identical nuclei restricts which rovibrational states may be combined with which total nuclear spin functions; forbidden combinations have zero statistical weight and the corresponding transitions vanish.","Two of his results rest entirely on this: the methyl-H spin statistics that made the CH2OH assignment unambiguous, and the complete disappearance of certain C60 transitions from the Bose statistics of 60 identical carbon-12 nuclei."],
["bosestat","Bose-Einstein statistics (in a molecule)","quantum","Carbon-12 nuclei are bosons, and in a perfectly symmetric cage of sixty of them, that fact alone deletes whole rotational transitions.","Sixty indistinguishable coins arranged on a perfectly symmetric board — some arrangements are literally the same arrangement, so they cannot be counted twice.","For identical bosons the total wavefunction must be symmetric under nuclear exchange; combined with icosahedral selection rules this forbids specific rovibrational transitions in carbon-12 C60.","The 2024-2025 result: observing the R(J = 0-29) progression at 8.4 microns and seeing the predicted transitions vanish is a direct, full-contrast confirmation of nuclear exchange statistics in a 60-atom molecule."],
["icosahedral","Icosahedral symmetry (Ih)","quantum","The highest symmetry a molecule can practically have — a soccer-ball cage with 120 symmetry operations.","A perfectly cut gemstone that looks the same from an unusually large number of directions.","The point group Ih, with sixty rotational and sixty improper operations, whose irreducible representations govern which rovibrational levels exist and which transitions are allowed in C60.","Icosahedral rovibrational fine structure was predicted for carbon-12 C60 in 1986 and had never been seen in any physical system until his collaboration observed it in 2023."],
["ergodicity","Ergodicity breaking","quantum","A system that fails to explore all the states it is energetically allowed to reach — it stays stuck in a corner of its own phase space.","A ball in a bowl that, for reasons of its own, keeps circling one rim instead of eventually visiting the whole surface.","Failure of a system's dynamics to sample the full accessible phase space consistent with its conserved quantities, so time averages do not equal ensemble averages.","The Science (2023) result: rapidly rotating C60 breaks rotational ergodicity, determined from its icosahedral rovibrational fine structure — without breaking symmetry. Relevant to protecting quantum coherence in complex systems."],
["eqq","Nuclear quadrupole coupling (eQq)","struct","A nucleus that is not perfectly spherical feels a torque from the lopsided electric field of its own molecule, which splits its spectral lines into a characteristic pattern.","A slightly egg-shaped compass needle sitting in an uneven magnetic field: it settles into particular tilts rather than any angle.","The interaction between a nuclear electric quadrupole moment Q and the electric field gradient q at the nucleus, parameterized as eQq; the resulting hyperfine pattern reports on the electronic environment and the orientation of the nucleus within the complex.","Your specialty, and a capability his infrared instrument does not have. Your N2-HCl versus N2-DCl work shows the averaged nitrogen-14 eQq changing on deuteration (about -4.529 MHz to a fitted average near -4.581 MHz) — a measurement of how zero-point motion reorients the molecule, which is precisely the kind of quantity he builds potentials to explain."],
["spinrot","Spin-rotation coupling","struct","In a molecule with an unpaired electron, the electron's magnetic moment feels the molecule's rotation, splitting levels further.","A gyroscope carried by a spinning carousel: the two rotations interact.","Coupling between electron spin angular momentum and molecular rotation, parameterized by a spin-rotation tensor, significant in open-shell radicals and in complexes containing O2 or NO.","Central to open-shell complexes — including the O2 van der Waals systems whose effective Hamiltonian you have been working through, and to his NO scattering work."],
["spinorbit","Spin-orbit coupling","quantum","An electron's spin and its orbital motion interact, so states that would otherwise be degenerate split into separate levels.","A spinning top whose wobble depends on which way it is also orbiting the room.","Relativistic coupling of electron spin and orbital angular momentum, producing fine-structure states such as the 2-Pi-one-half and 2-Pi-three-halves manifolds of NO.","His scattering work uses NO specifically because its spin-orbit states are resolvable, letting him detect non-adiabatic electronic excitation at a liquid surface."],
["rennerteller","Renner-Teller effect","quantum","In a bent-versus-linear molecule with electronic degeneracy, bending and electronic motion get entangled, distorting the level pattern.","A bicycle wheel that wobbles differently depending on which way it is leaning — lean and spin are not independent.","Coupling between electronic orbital angular momentum and the bending vibration in a linear molecule with a degenerate electronic state, lifting the degeneracy as the molecule bends.","Relevant to his work on open-shell linear and quasi-linear species such as the ethynyl radical's vibronic bands."],
["bornopp","Born-Oppenheimer approximation","theory","Treat the electrons as instantly adjusting to wherever the nuclei are, so you can talk about nuclei moving on a fixed energy surface.","Assuming the crew repositions so fast that the ship's captain can plan the route as if the crew were always already in place.","Separation of electronic and nuclear motion based on their mass disparity, giving potential energy surfaces on which nuclear dynamics occurs.","It underpins the whole idea of a potential energy surface — and its breakdown is the subject of his non-adiabatic scattering work, where electronic states change during a collision."],
["nonadiabatic","Non-adiabatic dynamics / surface hopping","iface","Molecules sometimes jump between different electronic states during a collision, instead of staying on one energy surface.","A car changing lanes mid-corner rather than following its lane all the way round.","Transitions between electronic potential energy surfaces where the Born-Oppenheimer separation fails, treated by surface-hopping or fully quantum coupled-channel methods.","He detected it at a liquid surface: NO scattering off room-temperature ionic liquids and off hot gallium emerges with changed spin-orbit state, meaning electronic degrees of freedom took part in the collision."],
["zpe","Zero-point energy and vibrational averaging","quantum","A molecule never sits still, even at absolute zero, so every measured structure is an average over that residual motion.","A photograph of a shivering person with a long exposure: you measure the blur-averaged outline, not the true one.","The lowest vibrational level retains energy of order one half h-nu per mode; observed rotational constants reflect expectation values over that vibrational wavefunction, not the equilibrium geometry.","Why deuteration changes measured constants at all — a heavier isotope has smaller zero-point amplitude and therefore a different average geometry. This is the key to explaining your own H-to-D eQq shift."],
["isotopologue","Isotopologue and isotopomer","struct","The same molecule with a heavier atom substituted (isotopologue), or with the isotopes placed differently within it (isotopomer).","The same chair built with one oak leg instead of pine (isotopologue) versus the oak leg moved to a different corner (isotopomer).","Isotopologues differ in isotopic composition; isotopomers share composition but differ in isotopic position. Both change moments of inertia without changing the potential energy surface.","The classic structure-determination tool: measuring many isotopologues of one complex overdetermines the geometry. Your eight-isotopologue N2-HCl study and his ND2H2+ / NH3D+ / ND3H+ series are the same strategy."],
["kraitchman","Kraitchman analysis","struct","Use the change in rotational constants on single-isotope substitution to pin down where that one atom sits.","Weighing a mobile before and after swapping one hanging piece for a heavier one, and deducing exactly where that piece hangs.","Substitution coordinates are obtained from the differences in principal moments of inertia between parent and singly-substituted isotopologues, yielding atom positions in the principal axis system.","The standard route from your kind of data to a geometry. He gets structure from vibrational band analysis; you get it from isotopic substitution. Two independent determinations of the same thing is a strong paper."],
["rigidbender","Rigid bender model","theory","A simplified treatment where a complex is allowed to bend freely but all other bonds are held fixed.","Modelling a hinged door as rigid panels and a flexible hinge, ignoring the flex of the wood.","A one- or two-dimensional Hamiltonian in the large-amplitude bending coordinate with the remaining coordinates held at relaxed or fixed values, used to extract the intermolecular bending potential.","He used rigid-bender analysis to extract the intermolecular bending potential of a hydrogen bond — a direct precursor to modern variational treatments."],
["effham","Effective Hamiltonian","theory","A compact set of fitted terms that reproduces the observed level pattern without solving the full problem from scratch.","A local bus timetable that predicts arrivals accurately without modelling traffic flow across the whole city.","A reduced operator acting within a chosen basis, with parameters absorbing the effect of remote states, fitted to experiment and chosen for physical interpretability.","Every paper's analysis layer — and the specific thing you have been learning for open-shell O2 complexes via the Qian/Howard formalism. You already speak this language."],
["branches","P, Q and R branches","spec","Groups of lines in a band where the molecule's rotation decreases, stays the same, or increases along with the vibrational jump.","Three staircases leaving the same landing: one down, one level, one up.","Transitions with change in J of minus one, zero and plus one respectively; which branches appear is governed by the band type and the symmetry selection rules.","The C60 analysis hinged on reading the R-branch progression from J equal zero upward and noticing which lines were missing. Branch structure is the first thing he reads off a new spectrum."],
["bandtype","a-, b- and c-type selection rules","spec","Which lines you see depends on which molecular axis the vibration shakes the dipole along.","Pushing a swing from the front, the side, or underneath — each produces a different pattern of motion.","Transition moment components along the a, b or c principal inertial axes give characteristic rotational selection rules and therefore distinctive band patterns.","Diagnostic for assignment: the phenyl work identified b-type structure, and the CH2OH assignment relied on recognizing A-type Ka equal zero and one subbands."],
["bandorigin","Band origin","spec","The single frequency that labels where a vibrational band sits, stripped of all rotational structure.","The address of a building, as opposed to the individual apartment numbers.","The pure vibrational term-value difference, obtained by fitting rotational structure and extrapolating to zero rotational angular momentum.","The headline number of a new spectrum. Cyclobutyl: 3068.7887(4) cm-1 — note the four-decimal precision and the quoted uncertainty. That precision is the product."],
["combdiff","Combination differences","spec","Compare pairs of lines that share an upper or lower level to extract energy spacings without knowing anything else.","Working out the height of a step by comparing two measurements that both include the same unknown offset, so the offset cancels.","Differences between transitions sharing a common level yield ground- or excited-state rotational energy spacings directly, independent of the band origin.","The bread-and-butter assignment check. If your combination differences do not close, your assignment is wrong — and he will ask whether you checked."],
["d0","Dissociation energy D0","struct","How much energy it actually takes to pull a weakly bound complex apart, measured from its lowest real level.","The height of the wall you must clear starting from where you are already standing, not from the floor.","The energy difference between the lowest bound rovibrational level and the dissociation asymptote, equal to the well depth less zero-point energy.","The number every potential surface must reproduce. In weakly bound complexes zero-point energy is a large fraction of the well depth, so D0 and well depth differ substantially."],
// ---------- species ----------
["radical","Free radical","species","A molecule with an unpaired electron — reactive, short-lived, and central to combustion, the atmosphere and interstellar chemistry.","A dancer without a partner, grabbing at whoever passes.","An open-shell species with a non-zero total electron spin, usually a doublet, with correspondingly rich fine and hyperfine structure.","The central cast of his spectroscopy programme. They cannot be bottled, so they must be made and measured in the same breath — which is what the discharge slit jet does."],
["carbene","Carbene (singlet and triplet)","species","A carbon with only two bonds and two spare electrons, which can pair them up or leave them apart — giving two chemically distinct versions.","A person with two free hands who either clasps them together or holds them apart; the two postures behave quite differently.","A divalent carbon species whose two non-bonding electrons occupy either the same orbital with opposed spins (singlet) or different orbitals with parallel spins (triplet), with different geometries and reactivity.","He has studied halocarbenes HCF and HCBr at high resolution, and singlet/triplet methylene sits in his intellectual lineage from the Berkeley era."],
["resstab","Resonance-stabilized radical","species","A radical whose unpaired electron is smeared over several atoms, which makes it unusually long-lived and important in soot formation.","A debt split among several people instead of falling on one — easier for everyone to carry.","A radical in which the unpaired electron is delocalized over a pi system, lowering its energy and reducing its reactivity; propargyl, allyl and benzyl are the canonical examples.","Propargyl, allyl and benzyl are all in his published record. They are the key intermediates in forming the first aromatic rings, and therefore in soot and PAH chemistry."],
["hoco","trans-HOCO radical","species","The fleeting intermediate that exists for an instant when a hydroxyl radical and carbon monoxide combine on the way to carbon dioxide.","The moment of handover in a relay race — brief, but it decides the outcome.","The HO-CO complex, a shallow minimum on the OH plus CO to H plus CO2 potential surface, observed in its trans conformer via the fundamental OH stretch.","Chang, Buckingham and Nesbitt measured it sub-Doppler. The OH plus CO reaction is the main carbon-monoxide sink in combustion and the atmosphere, so this intermediate is genuinely consequential."],
["ch2oh","CH2OH (hydroxymethyl) radical","species","Methanol with one hydrogen removed from the carbon — a key combustion and atmospheric intermediate with a floppy OH group.","A weather vane on a tilted roof: the OH can swing right over to the mirror-image position.","An open-shell radical with a large-amplitude COH torsional coordinate producing a tunneling-split pair of symmetry states.","Schuder, Wang, Chang and Nesbitt obtained the first infrared observation of transitions from both tunneling states, made in the jet by chlorine-atom-mediated hydrogen abstraction from methanol."],
["ammonium","Astrochemically relevant ammonium cations","species","Protonated ammonia and its deuterated variants — ions abundant in cold interstellar clouds whose spectra are needed to identify them in space.","Fingerprint cards for suspects astronomers keep spotting but cannot yet name.","Isotopically substituted NH4+ ions such as NH3D+, ND2H2+ and ND3H+, whose NH-stretch rovibrational transitions were measured for the first time in a slit-jet discharge.","A whole series of first observations from the group. Deuterium fractionation is a thermometer for cold interstellar chemistry, which is why these specific isotopologues matter."],
["c60","C60 (buckminsterfullerene)","species","Sixty carbon atoms on the vertices of a soccer-ball cage — the most symmetric large molecule available.","A geodesic dome made of exactly sixty identical struts.","An icosahedral (Ih) spherical-top molecule with a very small rotational constant, such that even at modest temperatures the most populated rotational levels carry hundreds of quanta.","The flagship collaboration with the Ye group: rovibrational quantum-state resolution, then collision-induced relaxation, then ergodicity breaking, then full-contrast icosahedral Bose-Einstein statistics at 30 K."],
["combustion","Combustion and PAH-formation chemistry","species","How hydrocarbon fuels actually burn, and how soot particles get built out of small radicals.","Tracing how a few bricks become a wall, one radical handshake at a time.","Networks of elementary radical reactions governing ignition, flame propagation and the growth of polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons toward soot.","The stated motivation for most of his hydrocarbon-radical work: cyclic radicals like cyclobutyl and cyclopentyl are central to the pyrolysis chemistry of cyclic fuels."],
["ism","Interstellar medium relevance","species","Cold, thin clouds between the stars where exotic ions and radicals survive long enough to be seen by radio and infrared telescopes.","A nearly empty, deeply cold warehouse where fragile things last for millennia.","Low-density, low-temperature regions where gas-phase ion-molecule chemistry dominates; identification requires laboratory rest frequencies of candidate species.","Laboratory spectroscopy is the lookup table astronomy depends on. His ammonium-cation work exists so that telescope data can be interpreted."],
// ---------- interfaces ----------
["gasliquid","Gas-liquid interface","iface","The boundary where a gas meets a liquid — thin, disordered, constantly moving, and where a great deal of real-world chemistry happens.","The surface of a cup of tea: not quite the tea, not quite the air, and where everything interesting evaporates from."," A molecularly thin, dynamically fluctuating region across which momentum, energy and mass are exchanged; relevant to aerosols, heterogeneous catalysis and atmospheric uptake.","The arena for his entire reaction-dynamics programme. Keeping it clean and continuously refreshed is the hard experimental problem."],
["is_td","Impulsive scattering (IS) versus trapping-desorption (TD)","iface","A molecule hitting a liquid either bounces off quickly, keeping a memory of how it arrived, or sinks in, forgets, and later leaves at the surface temperature.","A ball off a trampoline versus a ball into a sandpit that is later tossed back out.","Two limiting channels: direct few-collision scattering with incomplete energy accommodation, versus full thermal equilibration followed by desorption at the surface temperature. The branching fraction and the accommodation coefficient quantify them.","The organizing framework of the whole programme. Every scattering paper reports how the flux splits between these channels and why."],
["stis","Subthermal impulsive scattering (STIS)","iface","At low collision energies, molecules do not fully trap as expected — they leave colder than the surface, remembering their cold jet origin.","Expecting a snowball thrown into a warm room to melt, and finding it comes back out still icy.","A scattering channel in which low-energy projectiles undergo incomplete rotational warming from their jet-cooled value rather than accommodating to the surface temperature.","Livingston Large and Nesbitt's discovery with low-energy CO. It is a genuinely new channel that the standard two-channel picture does not contain — and he flagged it as potentially crucial for inelastic energy transfer generally."],
["hotspot","The 'hot-spot' model","iface","Impulsive collisions share energy with a small local patch of the liquid rather than with the surface as a whole.","Dropping a hot coal on a rug: a small scorched spot, not a uniformly warmer rug.","A model in which energy exchange during impulsive scattering is localized to a few surface molecules forming a transient high-energy region, rather than being delivered to a continuum bath.","His own framework for impulsive scattering. If you discuss his interface work, this is the vocabulary to use."],
["leverarm","'Lever arm' model","iface","A simple geometric picture of how a projectile's translational energy gets converted into rotation when it strikes a surface off-centre.","Hitting a door off-centre makes it swing; the further from the hinge, the more it turns.","A reduced model relating rotational excitation to the impact-parameter-dependent torque exerted during collision, parameterized by an effective lever arm length.","Used to explain DCl scattering across thermal and hyperthermal regimes. He likes models simple enough to explain in a sentence but quantitative enough to test."],
["rtil","Room-temperature ionic liquid (RTIL)","iface","A salt that is liquid at room temperature, with essentially no vapour pressure — so it can be used as a liquid surface inside a vacuum chamber.","Molten table salt that happens to be liquid on your desk and never evaporates.","Organic salts, typically imidazolium cations with fluorinated anions, with negligible vapour pressure and chemically tunable cation and anion structure.","Tunability is the attraction: BMIM-Cl, BMIM-BF4, BMIM-Tf2N and the [Cnmim][Tf2N] alkyl-chain series let him change one surface variable at a time."],
["microjet","Liquid microjet","iface","A hair-thin, fast-moving stream of liquid water in vacuum, giving a continuously fresh aqueous surface.","A fire-hose jet shrunk to the width of a hair, so the surface is always brand new.","A laminar liquid filament in vacuum providing a renewing surface for volatile liquids that could not otherwise be studied under vacuum conditions.","How Ryazanov and Nesbitt studied state-resolved evaporation of NO from liquid water — a hard experiment that gets aqueous surfaces into a vacuum chamber."],
["sam","Self-assembled monolayer (SAM)","iface","A single ordered layer of molecules that spontaneously arranges itself on a surface, giving a well-defined, tunable solid interface.","A field of grass where every blade stands the same way, planted by itself.","A monolayer of amphiphilic molecules chemisorbed onto a substrate with ordered tail-group packing, allowing systematic variation of surface chemistry and chain length.","Gives him a well-characterized, chemically tunable comparison surface against which liquid behaviour can be judged."],
["landauteller","Landau-Teller / Rapp theory","theory","Classic theory predicting how efficiently a collision transfers energy into vibration — generally very inefficiently unless the collision is fast and hard.","Pushing a heavy pendulum: a slow steady push does almost nothing, a sharp fast strike does much more.","Analytic models giving vibrational energy transfer probability as an exponential function of the ratio of collision duration to vibrational period.","He tested it directly at a liquid interface using OCS, a polyatomic chosen so rotational and vibrational channels could be separated."],
["accommodation","Accommodation coefficient (alpha)","iface","A single number saying what fraction of molecules fully equilibrate with a surface before leaving.","The fraction of visitors who actually sit down and stay a while, rather than turning round at the door.","The fraction of incident flux that thermally accommodates to the surface temperature, extracted from the trapping-desorption component of the scattered state distribution.","Quoted throughout the scattering papers — values of roughly 0.25 to 0.40 for NO on ionic liquids, meaning most molecules scatter impulsively rather than trapping."],
["stereo","Stereodynamics","iface","How the orientation and alignment of a molecule as it arrives changes what happens in the collision.","Whether a thrown hammer lands head-first or handle-first changes the dent it leaves.","The dependence of scattering or reaction outcome on the relative orientation of molecular axes and angular momentum vectors with respect to the collision frame.","He studies state-resolved scattering as a function of incident angle and quantum state, so orientation effects are directly observable rather than averaged away."],
// ---------- nano ----------
["qdot","Colloidal quantum dot","nano","A nanometre-scale semiconductor crystal whose colour is set purely by its size, because the electrons are squeezed into a box.","A guitar string: shorten it and the pitch rises. Shrink the dot and the emission blue-shifts.","A nanocrystal smaller than the exciton Bohr radius, in which quantum confinement discretizes the electronic levels and makes the band gap size-dependent.","The subject of his most-cited work. CdSe dots are the model system for everything he found about blinking."],
["blinking","Blinking / fluorescence intermittency","nano","A single quantum dot under constant illumination randomly switches between emitting brightly and going dark, for stretches lasting microseconds to minutes.","A streetlamp with a faulty connection that flickers on wildly unpredictable schedules.","Stochastic switching between emissive and non-emissive states, attributed to charging and trapping processes; on- and off-time durations follow power-law rather than exponential statistics.","Kuno, Fromm, Hamann, Gallagher and Nesbitt (2000) established the universal power law — over a thousand citations and a whole literature in response."],
["powerlaw","Power-law blinking statistics","nano","The dark periods have no characteristic length: there are many short ones, fewer long ones, and the pattern looks the same at every timescale.","Earthquakes — countless tiny ones, rare enormous ones, and no typical size.","Probability densities of on- and off-durations scaling as a power of time rather than decaying exponentially, implying a broad distribution of underlying rates rather than one kinetic process.","The surprise that made the result famous. Simple kinetics predicts an exponential; the data refused, and explaining why took the field fifteen years."],
["auger","Auger ionization and biexcitons","nano","When two excitations sit in one tiny dot, one can give all its energy to the other instead of emitting light, ejecting a charge and turning the dot dark.","Two people in a phone box: one shoves the other out the door rather than both leaving politely.","Non-radiative Auger recombination of a biexciton transfers energy to a single carrier, promoting it above the confinement barrier and leaving the dot charged and non-emissive.","Peterson and Nesbitt traced modified power-law behaviour to this mechanism — moving blinking from phenomenology to mechanism."],
["plasmon","Plasmon / localized surface plasmon resonance","nano","Light makes all the free electrons in a metal nanoparticle slosh back and forth together, concentrating the optical field into a tiny volume.","A crowd in a stadium doing a coordinated wave, far bigger than any individual's motion.","A collective oscillation of conduction electrons in a metallic nanostructure, resonant at a geometry- and material-dependent frequency, producing strong local field enhancement.","The basis of his plasmonic nanoscience: hot electrons, photoemission and field enhancement all follow from the plasmon."],
["nearfield","Near-field enhancement","nano","Right at the tip or gap of a metal nanostructure, the optical field is many times stronger than the incoming light.","Water flowing gently in a river but blasting through a narrow gap between two rocks.","Sub-wavelength spatial concentration of electromagnetic energy at nanostructure features, with enhancement factors that can be orders of magnitude in intensity.","His single-nanorod work showed that it is near-field enhancement, not far-field intensity, that controls multiphoton photoelectron emission — and that it is polarization-controlled and directional."],
["hotcarrier","Hot electrons / hot carriers","nano","Right after a plasmon decays, the electrons are far hotter than the metal and briefly carry much more energy than usual — if you can catch them in time.","Sparks from a struck flint: intensely hot for an instant, then gone.","Non-thermal carrier distributions produced by plasmon decay, which relax by electron-electron and then electron-phonon scattering on femtosecond to picosecond timescales.","His femtosecond work resolves these dynamics in single nanoparticles with simultaneous time, angle and energy resolution — the key question being whether they can be harvested before thermalizing."],
["strongcoupling","Strong coupling / Rabi splitting","nano","When a single emitter and an optical cavity exchange energy faster than either loses it, light and matter stop being separable things.","Two pendulums joined by a stiff spring: you can no longer describe one without the other.","Coherent light-matter coupling exceeding the dissipation rates, producing hybridized polariton states separated by the Rabi splitting.","Room-temperature strong coupling of a single colloidal dot to a scanning-probe plasmonic nanoresonator, with Rabi splittings up to about 110 meV."],
["graphene","Graphene plasmonics and quantum dots","nano","A one-atom-thick carbon sheet whose optical and electronic behaviour can be tuned electrically, and patterned into nanoscale constrictions.","A sheet of chicken wire one atom thick, whose conductivity you can change with a dial.","A two-dimensional semimetal with a linear dispersion and electrically tunable Fermi level; patterning a nanoconstriction opens a quantum-confinement gap.","Appears in his nanoscience orbit via nanostructured graphene for hot-electron detection and single-plasmon electrical detection proposals."],
// ---------- biophysics ----------
["fret","Forster radius (R0)","bio","The distance at which energy transfer between two dyes is exactly fifty per cent efficient — the ruler's calibration mark.","The point on a dimmer switch where the light is at half brightness.","The donor-acceptor separation at which transfer efficiency is one half, determined by spectral overlap, quantum yield, refractive index and relative dipole orientation; typically 5-6 nm for Cy3-Cy5.","It sets the window of distances smFRET can see. Choosing a dye pair whose R0 matches the conformational change you expect is half the experimental design."],
["riboswitch","Riboswitch","bio","A stretch of RNA that physically changes shape when it binds a small molecule, switching a gene on or off without any protein involved.","A turnstile that only unlocks when you drop in the right coin.","A cis-acting regulatory RNA element whose ligand-bound and unbound conformations differentially permit transcription or translation of the downstream gene.","A major system in his biophysics programme — preQ1, Mn2+, lysine and nickel/cobalt riboswitches. They are also candidate antibiotic targets, which is the stated applied motivation."],
["tetraloop","GAAA tetraloop-receptor interaction","bio","A small, well-studied RNA handshake: a four-nucleotide loop docking into a matching pocket. The cleanest single tertiary contact available.","A single standardized plug-and-socket pair, isolated from all the rest of the wiring.","A ubiquitous RNA tertiary motif in which a GNRA-type tetraloop docks into an eleven-nucleotide receptor, usable as an isolated two-state folding system.","His workhorse for isolating one tertiary interaction. Using it is what let the crowding measurements be interpreted unambiguously."],
["pseudoknot","Pseudoknot","bio","An RNA fold where a loop pairs with a sequence outside itself, threading the strand through in a knot-like topology.","Shoelaces where one lace is threaded back through a loop of the other.","A tertiary structure in which bases within a loop pair with a complementary region outside the stem-loop, producing interleaved helices.","The human telomerase RNA pseudoknot is one of his systems, and the preQ1 riboswitch is H-type pseudoknotted. Pseudoknots are hard to predict computationally, so measurement matters."],
["crowding","Molecular crowding","bio","The inside of a cell is packed with other molecules, so there is far less free space than in a test tube, which pushes folded shapes to be compact.","A packed train carriage: you fold your arms not by choice but because there is no room to spread out.","Excluded-volume and depletion effects from high cosolute concentrations that shift conformational equilibria toward compact states and alter folding kinetics.","One of his most-cited biophysics results: under cell-like crowding, single RNA molecules folded roughly 35 times faster than in dilute solution. And smaller crowders crowd more effectively, which excluded volume alone does not explain."],
["depletion","Depletion force","bio","Two surfaces attract each other because crowders cannot fit in the gap between them, so there is a pressure imbalance pushing them together.","Two boats drifting together in choppy water because the waves between them are smaller than those outside.","An entropic attraction arising from the exclusion of cosolute particles from the region between two surfaces, with magnitude depending on crowder size and concentration.","The model that explained why smaller crowders crowd better — the size dependence is the signature of depletion rather than simple volume exclusion."],
["inducedfit","Induced fit versus conformational selection","bio","Either the ligand binds first and then the molecule changes shape, or the molecule happens into the right shape first and then the ligand binds.","Either the key reshapes the lock, or you wait for the lock to click into the shape your key already fits.","Two limiting kinetic mechanisms for ligand-coupled conformational change, distinguishable by the ligand-concentration dependence of the observed rate constants.","Sung and Nesbitt showed that magnesium acts allosterically on the Mn2+ riboswitch to switch between these pathways. Distinguishing them requires the single-molecule rates, not just equilibrium constants — which is the argument for the whole technique."],
["osmolyte","Osmolyte","bio","A small solute cells accumulate to cope with stress, which also happens to stabilize or destabilize folded structures.","Antifreeze in a radiator: present for one reason, with side effects on everything in the system.","Small organic solutes such as urea, betaine or trimethylamine N-oxide that shift biomolecular folding equilibria through preferential interaction or exclusion.","Holmstrom, Dupuis and Nesbitt separated kinetic from thermodynamic origins of osmolyte effects — and amino-acid-specific effects on RNA tertiary interactions are a continuing thread."],
["vanthoff","van 't Hoff, Arrhenius and Eyring analysis","theory","Measure a rate or equilibrium constant at several temperatures and the slope tells you the energy and entropy behind it.","Timing how long bread takes to rise at several oven settings, and deducing the chemistry from the pattern.","Plots of the logarithm of an equilibrium or rate constant against inverse temperature yield enthalpy, entropy and activation parameters via the van 't Hoff, Arrhenius and Eyring relations.","The reason his group bothered to build temperature control into a single-molecule microscope: it converts a kinetics instrument into a thermodynamics instrument."],
["maxwell","Maxwell relation (at the single-molecule level)","theory","An exact thermodynamic identity linking how volume changes with temperature to how entropy changes with pressure — testable, not assumed.","Two different routes up the same hill must end at the same altitude; if they do not, your map is wrong.","Equalities among second derivatives of a thermodynamic potential, such as the derivative of volume with respect to temperature equalling minus the derivative of entropy with respect to pressure.","Sung and Nesbitt's 2025 two-dimensional pressure/temperature smFRET study validates such a relation one molecule at a time. It is a striking claim: textbook bulk thermodynamics holding for a single molecule."],
["heatcap","Excess heat capacity of folding","bio","How much the energy cost of folding itself changes with temperature — a second-order quantity most techniques cannot reach.","Not just how steep the hill is, but how the steepness changes as you climb.","The difference in heat capacity between folded and unfolded states, obtained from the curvature of van 't Hoff plots rather than their slope.","Measuring it at the single-molecule level requires exceptional data quality over a wide temperature range. Doing it at all is a statement about the instrument."],
["activationvolume","Activation and reaction volumes","bio","How much the molecule's effective volume changes as it folds or passes through its transition state, measured by squeezing it.","Judging how much a sponge can compress by pressing it, rather than by looking at it.","Volume changes obtained from the pressure dependence of rate and equilibrium constants, through the derivative of the logarithm of the constant with respect to pressure.","Pressure gives an orthogonal thermodynamic axis to temperature. Combining both is what makes the two-dimensional P/T experiment possible."],
["hmm","Hidden Markov analysis of trajectories","theory","An algorithm that finds the discrete hidden states a noisy time trace is hopping between, and the rates between them.","Working out a person's unseen daily routine purely from which lights are on in the house.","A probabilistic model inferring the most likely sequence of discrete hidden states and the associated transition rate matrix from noisy observations.","The standard analysis for smFRET trajectories, and a natural entry point if your strength is data analysis rather than optics."],
// ---------- instrumentation / general ----------
["lockin","Lock-in detection","inst","Modulate your signal at a known frequency and look only at that frequency, so almost all the noise is discarded.","Picking out one specific whistled note in a noisy street because you know exactly which note to listen for.","Phase-sensitive detection that multiplies the input by a reference at the modulation frequency and low-pass filters, dramatically narrowing the effective detection bandwidth.","The reason absorbances of a part in a million are measurable. Combined with concentration modulation, it is what makes radical detection possible at all."],
["transfercavity","Transfer cavity / HeNe stabilization","inst","A stable mirror cavity acts as a ruler for laser frequency, itself held steady against a reference laser.","A tuning fork that keeps the piano in tune, with the fork itself checked against a master fork.","A Fabry-Perot etalon whose length is locked to a polarization-stabilized helium-neon laser, providing evenly spaced transmission fringes for interpolating laser frequency.","How absolute frequency accuracy is achieved in his spectrometers, including in the new OPO where the signal wave is locked to such a cavity."],
["finesse","Cavity finesse","inst","How many times light bounces inside a mirror cavity before leaking out — more bounces means more absorption from the same sample.","How many laps a runner completes on a track before leaving; more laps, more distance covered in the same space.","A measure of cavity quality set by mirror reflectivity and loss, proportional to the number of round trips and hence to the effective absorption path length.","The sensitivity multiplier behind cavity-enhanced comb spectroscopy, and the reason trace breath biomarkers are detectable at all."],
["neas","Noise-equivalent absorption sensitivity","inst","The smallest absorption you could detect in one second of averaging — the honest figure of merit for a spectrometer.","The faintest sound a microphone can register above its own hiss.","The absorption coefficient giving unity signal-to-noise in a one-hertz detection bandwidth, typically quoted in inverse centimetres per root hertz.","He quotes it, because a NIST scientist quotes limits rather than claims. Around 1e-7 per root hertz for the slit-jet instruments."],
["nitrogen","NITROGEN (the software)","theory","A Python package for building potential energy surfaces and solving for vibrational wavefunctions in molecules with awkward, large-amplitude coordinates.","A flight simulator for nuclear motion: define the terrain, then fly the wavefunction over it.","A nuclear-motion code supporting curvilinear coordinate definitions, discrete-variable representations and variational solutions for vibration-rotation problems where rigid-rotor approximations fail.","Written by Bryan Changala — who is a co-author on your Ne-HCl work and on Nesbitt's C60 Science paper. Your fluency with this tool, applied to his puckering and torsional problems, is your single sharpest technical offer."],
["abinitio","Ab initio electronic structure","theory","Solving the electronic Schrodinger equation from scratch to predict geometry, frequencies and interaction energies.","Computing a bridge's load capacity from the physics of steel rather than from how similar bridges behaved.","Wavefunction- or density-based methods such as coupled-cluster theory, used with large basis sets and extrapolation to approximate the exact electronic energy.","Every modern paper of his pairs experiment with computation — frequently with external theory partners. The experiment anchors the calculation and the calculation interprets the experiment."],
["breath","Breath analysis / exhaled biomarkers","inst","Reading disease signatures in the trace gases people breathe out, without taking blood.","Diagnosing what is cooking in a kitchen from the smell in the hallway.","Quantitative detection of trace volatile biomarkers in exhaled air, requiring part-per-billion sensitivity, broad species coverage and real-time analysis.","His Ye-group collaboration pushed comb-based breath sensitivity up roughly a thousandfold, measuring four biomarkers at once with more in reach, and later detecting SARS-CoV-2 infection. The most translational thing he does."]
];
